1.text:
The five words that are given as positive examples are all CVC words whose
vowel is one of { a , u , o } and whose final consonant is one of the
final consonants that actually occurs in the positive set

* dad  d a d
* dab  d a b
* gut  g u t
* low  l o w
* cat  c a t

i.e. V ∈{ a,u,o } and C3 ∈{ d,b,t,w }.  

That is the description that is satisfied by the five positive
examples and that is violated by the five negative examples that are
quoted

* ear V=a but C3=r∉{d,b,t,w}
* cop V=o but C3=p∉{d,b,t,w}
* ego V∉{a,u,o}
* mom V=o but C3=m∉{d,b,t,w}
* ate V∉{a,u,o}

On that description

* leg = l e g  V=e∉{a,u,o} and C3=g∉{d,b,t,w}
* dam = d a m  C3=m∉{d,b,t,w}
* rat = r a t  V=a, C3=t – the only option that satisfies V∈{a,u,o}
  and C3∈{d,b,t,w}
* car = c a r  C3=r∉{d,b,t,w}
* bin = b i n  V=i∉{a,u,o}

so that, up to the usual formulation of the problem, rat is the only
member of the list that belongs to the  V∈{a,u,o}, C3∈{d,b,t,w}  family
and e.g. leg is the first clear violation of the family.  In particular
leg is a CVC word with the forbidden vowel e and the forbidden final
consonant g, so it is not a member of the dad–dab–gut–low–cat family.
The other obvious violations are dam, car and bin; rat is the unique
option that does satisfy the V∈{a,u,o}, C3∈{d,b,t,w} description.

